{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-128335" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f$ defined by $f(x)=\\frac{7}{2{x}^{2}-5x-3}.$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nCalculate ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${f}^{′}(x)\\,=\\,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043928/state.json" "file_sha256" "d71e9e6a8f1a197bb35a43071b7a315e8aa376a90531964c9b5918bf2ceb0e10"} {"question" "q-128335" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The numerator and denominator are $u(x)=7$ and $v(x)=2{x}^{2}-5x-3,$ respectively. Their derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=0,\\,{v}^{′}(x)=4x-5.\n$$\n\nApplying the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x){v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0)\\cdot (2{x}^{2}-5x-3)-7\\cdot (4x-5)}{{(2{x}^{2}-5x-3)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-7\\cdot (4x-5)}{{(2{x}^{2}-5x-3)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7(5-4x)}{{(2{x}^{2}-5x-3)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043928/state.json" "file_sha256" "d71e9e6a8f1a197bb35a43071b7a315e8aa376a90531964c9b5918bf2ceb0e10"} {"question" "q-128335" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043928/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2ce0c9543a727a436f061c6b62155cdd469cd2cb2a713ca1d41d5cfa55fc542f"} {"question" "q-128335" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043928/state.json" "file_sha256" "d71e9e6a8f1a197bb35a43071b7a315e8aa376a90531964c9b5918bf2ceb0e10"} {"question" "q-128335" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{-7(4x-5)}{(2x^2-5x-3)^2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043928/state.json" "file_sha256" "d71e9e6a8f1a197bb35a43071b7a315e8aa376a90531964c9b5918bf2ceb0e10"} {"question" "q-128332" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f$ defined by $f(x)=\\frac{7}{2{x}^{2}-5x-3}.$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nCalculate ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${f}^{′}(x)\\,=\\,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{-7(4x-5)}{(2x^2-5x-3)^2}$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nThe slope of the tangent line to the graph of $y=f(x)$ at $x=2$ is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043928/state.json" "file_sha256" "d71e9e6a8f1a197bb35a43071b7a315e8aa376a90531964c9b5918bf2ceb0e10"} {"question" "q-128332" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The slope of the tangent at $x=2$ is equal to ${f}^{′}(2).$ In part 1, we found\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{7(5-4x)}{(2{x}^{2}-5x-3)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate at $x=2,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{7(5-4\\cdot 2)}{(2\\cdot {2}^{2}-5\\cdot 2-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\cdot (-3)}{(-5)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{21}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line to the curve at $x=2$ is $-\\frac{21}{25}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043928/state.json" "file_sha256" "d71e9e6a8f1a197bb35a43071b7a315e8aa376a90531964c9b5918bf2ceb0e10"} {"question" "q-128332" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043928/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2ce0c9543a727a436f061c6b62155cdd469cd2cb2a713ca1d41d5cfa55fc542f"} {"question" "q-128332" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043928/state.json" "file_sha256" "d71e9e6a8f1a197bb35a43071b7a315e8aa376a90531964c9b5918bf2ceb0e10"} {"question" "q-128332" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{21}{25}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043928/state.json" "file_sha256" "d71e9e6a8f1a197bb35a43071b7a315e8aa376a90531964c9b5918bf2ceb0e10"} {"question" "q-128660" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f$ defined by $f(x)=\\frac{7}{2{x}^{2}-5x-3}.$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nCalculate ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${f}^{′}(x)\\,=\\,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{-7(4x-5)}{(2x^2-5x-3)^2}$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nThe slope of the tangent line to the graph of $y=f(x)$ at $x=2$ is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\frac{21}{25}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nCurrent part:\n\nConsider a function $g(x)$ such that $g(2)=-2$ and ${g}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{2}.$ If $h(x)=\\frac{5-25f(x)}{g(x)+3},$ then ${h}^{′}(2)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043928/state.json" "file_sha256" "d71e9e6a8f1a197bb35a43071b7a315e8aa376a90531964c9b5918bf2ceb0e10"} {"question" "q-128660" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "In part 2 we found that ${f}^{′}(2)=-\\frac{21}{25}.$ Also,\n\n$$\nf(2)=\\frac{7}{2\\cdot {2}^{2}-5\\cdot 2-3}=-\\frac{7}{5}.\n$$\n\nThe quotient rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{u}{v})=\\frac{{u}^{′}v-u{v}^{′}}{{v}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=5-25f(x)$ and $v=g(x)+3.$ Differentiating each, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-25{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the quotient rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}v-u{v}^{′}}{{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-25{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot (g(x)+3)-(5-25f(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x)+3)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $x=2,$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{-25{f}^{′}(2)\\cdot (g(2)+3)-(5-25f(2))\\cdot {g}^{′}(2)}{(g(2)+3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-25\\cdot (-\\frac{21}{25})\\cdot (-2+3)-(5-25\\cdot (-\\frac{7}{5}))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}}{(-2+3)^{2}} \\\\  & =21-20 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043928/state.json" "file_sha256" "d71e9e6a8f1a197bb35a43071b7a315e8aa376a90531964c9b5918bf2ceb0e10"} {"question" "q-128660" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043928/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2ce0c9543a727a436f061c6b62155cdd469cd2cb2a713ca1d41d5cfa55fc542f"} {"question" "q-128660" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043928/state.json" "file_sha256" "d71e9e6a8f1a197bb35a43071b7a315e8aa376a90531964c9b5918bf2ceb0e10"} {"question" "q-128660" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043928/state.json" "file_sha256" "d71e9e6a8f1a197bb35a43071b7a315e8aa376a90531964c9b5918bf2ceb0e10"} {"question" "q-128333" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f$ defined by $f(x)=\\frac{7}{2{x}^{2}-5x-3}.$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nCalculate ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${f}^{′}(x)\\,=\\,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{-7(4x-5)}{(2x^2-5x-3)^2}$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nThe slope of the tangent line to the graph of $y=f(x)$ at $x=2$ is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\frac{21}{25}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nConsider a function $g(x)$ such that $g(2)=-2$ and ${g}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{2}.$ If $h(x)=\\frac{5-25f(x)}{g(x)+3},$ then ${h}^{′}(2)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $1$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nCurrent part:\n\nAt which values of $x\\in (-\\frac{1}{2},3)$ does $f$ have a critical point?\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The function $f$ has a critical point at $x=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043928/state.json" "file_sha256" "d71e9e6a8f1a197bb35a43071b7a315e8aa376a90531964c9b5918bf2ceb0e10"} {"question" "q-128333" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nIn part 1, we obtained\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{7(5-4x)}{(2{x}^{2}-5x-3)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n* First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7(5-4x)}{(2{x}^{2}-5x-3)^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 7(5-4x) & =0 \\\\ 5-4x & =0 \\\\ 4x & =5 \\\\ x & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has a stationary point at $x=\\frac{5}{4}.$\n\n* Second, we find the points in the domain of $f(x)$ where ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist. Factoring our expression for ${f}^{′}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{7(5-4x)}{[(2x+1)(x-3)]^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist at $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=3.$ But these points do not lie in the interval $(-\\frac{1}{2},3).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the only critical point of $f(x)$ is $x=\\frac{5}{4}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043928/state.json" "file_sha256" "d71e9e6a8f1a197bb35a43071b7a315e8aa376a90531964c9b5918bf2ceb0e10"} {"question" "q-128333" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043928/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2ce0c9543a727a436f061c6b62155cdd469cd2cb2a713ca1d41d5cfa55fc542f"} {"question" "q-128333" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043928/state.json" "file_sha256" "d71e9e6a8f1a197bb35a43071b7a315e8aa376a90531964c9b5918bf2ceb0e10"} {"question" "q-128333" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{5}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043928/state.json" "file_sha256" "d71e9e6a8f1a197bb35a43071b7a315e8aa376a90531964c9b5918bf2ceb0e10"} {"question" "q-128336" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f$ defined by $f(x)=\\frac{7}{2{x}^{2}-5x-3}.$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nCalculate ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${f}^{′}(x)\\,=\\,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{-7(4x-5)}{(2x^2-5x-3)^2}$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nThe slope of the tangent line to the graph of $y=f(x)$ at $x=2$ is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\frac{21}{25}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nConsider a function $g(x)$ such that $g(2)=-2$ and ${g}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{2}.$ If $h(x)=\\frac{5-25f(x)}{g(x)+3},$ then ${h}^{′}(2)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $1$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nAt which values of $x\\in (-\\frac{1}{2},3)$ does $f$ have a critical point?\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The function $f$ has a critical point at $x=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{5}{4}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nCurrent part:\n\nDetermine the number of local maxima and minima $f(x)$ has on the interval  $x\\in (-\\frac{1}{2},3).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $f(x)$ has \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,$ local maxima and \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,$ local minima." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043928/state.json" "file_sha256" "d71e9e6a8f1a197bb35a43071b7a315e8aa376a90531964c9b5918bf2ceb0e10"} {"question" "q-128336" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We have the derivative \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{7(4x-5)}{(2{x}^{2}-5x-3)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the critical point $x=\\frac{5}{4}.$ We use the first derivative test to determine whether this point is a local maximum or minimum.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=0,2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & =-\\frac{7(4\\cdot 0-5)}{(2\\cdot {0}^{2}-5\\cdot 0-3)^{2}}=\\frac{35}{9}>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =-\\frac{7(4\\cdot 2-5)}{(2\\cdot {2}^{2}-5\\cdot 2-3)^{2}}=-\\frac{21}{25}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in the table shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{5}{4})$ | $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $(\\frac{5}{4},3)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $0$ |  | $2$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\n \nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical point.\n\n* At $x=\\frac{5}{4},$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=\\frac{5}{4}$ is a local maximum.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)$ has $1$ local maximum and $0$ local minima on the interval $x\\in (-\\frac{1}{2},3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043928/state.json" "file_sha256" "d71e9e6a8f1a197bb35a43071b7a315e8aa376a90531964c9b5918bf2ceb0e10"} {"question" "q-128336" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043928/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2ce0c9543a727a436f061c6b62155cdd469cd2cb2a713ca1d41d5cfa55fc542f"} {"question" "q-128336" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043928/state.json" "file_sha256" "d71e9e6a8f1a197bb35a43071b7a315e8aa376a90531964c9b5918bf2ceb0e10"} {"question" "q-128336" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043928/state.json" "file_sha256" "d71e9e6a8f1a197bb35a43071b7a315e8aa376a90531964c9b5918bf2ceb0e10"} {"question" "q-128336" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043928/state.json" "file_sha256" "d71e9e6a8f1a197bb35a43071b7a315e8aa376a90531964c9b5918bf2ceb0e10"} {"question" "q-128336" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043928/state.json" "file_sha256" "d71e9e6a8f1a197bb35a43071b7a315e8aa376a90531964c9b5918bf2ceb0e10"} {"question" "q-128339" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f$ defined by $f(x)=\\frac{7}{2{x}^{2}-5x-3}.$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nCalculate ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${f}^{′}(x)\\,=\\,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{-7(4x-5)}{(2x^2-5x-3)^2}$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nThe slope of the tangent line to the graph of $y=f(x)$ at $x=2$ is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\frac{21}{25}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nConsider a function $g(x)$ such that $g(2)=-2$ and ${g}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{2}.$ If $h(x)=\\frac{5-25f(x)}{g(x)+3},$ then ${h}^{′}(2)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $1$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nAt which values of $x\\in (-\\frac{1}{2},3)$ does $f$ have a critical point?\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The function $f$ has a critical point at $x=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{5}{4}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nDetermine the number of local maxima and minima $f(x)$ has on the interval  $x\\in (-\\frac{1}{2},3).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $f(x)$ has \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $1$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,$ local maxima and \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $0$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,$ local minima.\n\nCurrent part:\n\nGiven that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=A{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x-3}+B{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{2x+1}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants, find $A$ and $B.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $A=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $B=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043928/state.json" "file_sha256" "d71e9e6a8f1a197bb35a43071b7a315e8aa376a90531964c9b5918bf2ceb0e10"} {"question" "q-128339" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we factor the numerator and denominator of $f(x)$:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{7}{2{x}^{2}-5x-3}=\\frac{7}{(x-3)(2x+1)}\n$$\n\nOur task is to find $A$ and $B$ such that\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{(x-3)(2x+1)}=\\frac{A}{x-3}+\\frac{B}{2x+1}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $(x-3)(2x+1)$ gives\n\n$$\n7=A(2x+1)+B(x-3).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-\\frac{1}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7 & =A(2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})+1)+B(-\\frac{1}{2}-3) \\\\ 7 & =-\\frac{7}{2}B \\\\ B & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7 & =A(2\\cdot 3+1)+B(3-3) \\\\ 7 & =7A \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{(x-3)(2x+1)}=\\frac{1}{x-3}-\\frac{2}{2x+1}.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }(\\frac{1}{x-3}-\\frac{2}{2x+1})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x-3}-2{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{2x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $A=1$ and $B=-2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043928/state.json" "file_sha256" "d71e9e6a8f1a197bb35a43071b7a315e8aa376a90531964c9b5918bf2ceb0e10"} {"question" "q-128339" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "categor